    "mapHeading": "Interactive map",
    "introHeading": "Water tenure in the Sample River Basin",
    "intro": "Water tenure is the set of relationships, formal or not, through which people and institutions gain, hold and lose access to water. In the fictional Sample River Basin, a basin agency operates a weir and a main canal, a registered water user association rotates a canal share, tail-end farmers rent pumps, an upland hamlet follows a customary steward at its spring, and households fish and cut reeds where no rule reaches.\n\nThe map shows these arrangements as tenure records classified on the FAO water tenure typology. Each record carries a confidence grade and source codes. Records marked as sensitive are shown at a deliberately displaced position.\n\n**Everything on this site is a synthetic example, not real data.**",
    "introLink": { "label": "Read more", "href": "/about" },
    "galleryCount": 4,
    "galleryLink": { "label": "More pictures", "href": "/gallery" }
